<div class="psf-doc">

    <h1>Sale Filter - How It Works</h1>
    <div class="psf-meta">
        <strong>Module:</strong> Panth_SaleFilter v<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($block->getModuleVersion()) ?>
        &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
        <strong>Hyvä Adapter:</strong> Panth_SaleFilterHyva v<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($block->getHyvaModuleVersion()) ?>
        &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
        <strong>Last reindex:</strong> see <a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getIndexerUrl()) ?>">Index Management</a>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Panth Sale Filter</strong> adds an <em>"On Sale"</em> option to the
        storefront's layered navigation on every category and search-result page.
        Unlike the stock "price decline" hint, this filter looks at the full pricing
        pipeline (catalog rules, special prices, date windows, per-group scoping)
        and caches the result in a dedicated index table so the sidebar stays fast
        even on very large catalogs.
    </p>

    <div class="psf-toc">
        <h3>Contents</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><a href="#psf-what">What it does</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-sources">Data sources &mdash; what counts as "on sale"</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-groups">Per-customer-group behavior</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-cache">Cache behavior</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-indexer">Indexer modes &amp; reindex triggers</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-storefront">Storefront rendering</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-config">Configuration reference</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-cli">Console commands</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-schema">Database schema</a></li>
            <li><a href="#psf-troubleshooting">Troubleshooting &amp; FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-what">1. What it does</h2>
    <p>
        The module ships three pieces that work together:
    </p>
    <table class="psf-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th style="width:22%">Piece</th><th style="width:28%">Purpose</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Indexer</strong></td>
                <td>Builds a flat table of <em>(product, website, group)</em> rows flagged on-sale / regular.</td>
                <td><code>panth_salefilter_product_index</code> (MView-driven, refreshed on rule / product / special-price change)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Layered-nav filter</strong></td>
                <td>Adds the "On Sale" option to the sidebar on category + search pages.</td>
                <td><code>\Panth\SaleFilter\Model\Layer\Filter\SaleFilter</code> (registered via <code>FilterListPlugin</code>)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Apply plugin</strong></td>
                <td>When <code>?sale_filter=1</code> or <code>?sale_filter=0</code> is in the URL, restricts the product collection so the grid + pager + toolbar count are all consistent.</td>
                <td><code>\Panth\SaleFilter\Plugin\Catalog\Model\Layer\ApplySaleFilterPlugin</code></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="psf-callout psf-callout-info">
        <strong>Why an index?</strong> Computing "is this product on sale right now for this group?" on every page render would re-evaluate the catalog-rule + special-price pipeline for every product in the collection &mdash; too slow for category pages with hundreds of items. The index pre-computes the answer, so the storefront reads a single boolean column.
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-sources">2. Data sources &mdash; what counts as "on sale"</h2>

    <p>The indexer considers a product on-sale when any of the following apply:</p>

    <table class="psf-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width:22%">Source</th>
                <th style="width:12%">Supported</th>
                <th style="width:26%">Scope</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Catalog Price Rules</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-yes">Yes</span></td>
                <td>per customer group, per website, date-windowed</td>
                <td>Reads <code>catalogrule_product_price</code>. Respects rule priority, <em>Stop Rules Processing</em>, <em>from/to</em> dates, and <em>Customer Groups</em> selection.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Special Price</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-yes">Yes</span></td>
                <td>website-scoped (applies to all groups on that website)</td>
                <td>Requires <code>special_price &gt; 0</code> AND <code>special_price &lt; price</code>. Respects <em>Special Price From/To Date</em> windows.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Configurable rollup</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-yes">Yes</span></td>
                <td>parent inherits if any child is on-sale</td>
                <td>If a single child variant (<code>MH01-XS-Black</code>) has a rule or special price, the parent configurable (<code>MH01</code>) is flagged on-sale so it appears in the filtered category listing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Tier Price / Group Price</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-no">Not tracked</span></td>
                <td>&mdash;</td>
                <td>The "Customer Group Price" table in <em>Advanced Pricing</em> is <strong>not</strong> currently considered. A product whose only discount is a per-group tier price will show under "No" even for the discounted group. Catalog rules are the recommended way to create per-group sales.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Disabled products</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-info">Excluded</span></td>
                <td>&mdash;</td>
                <td>Products with <em>Status = Disabled</em> or <em>Visibility = Not Visible Individually</em> are skipped by the storefront layer regardless of index state.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Out-of-stock products</strong></td>
                <td><span class="psf-tag psf-tag-info">Honors store config</span></td>
                <td>&mdash;</td>
                <td>Respects <em>Stores &gt; Configuration &gt; Catalog &gt; Inventory &gt; Display Out of Stock Products</em>. Out-of-stock items are hidden from the filter when that setting is <em>No</em>.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="psf-callout">
        <strong>Example scenario.</strong> A catalog rule <em>"Members Only 10% Off"</em> for groups [General, Wholesale, Retailer] on SKU <code>MH01-XS-Black</code> is saved. After reindex the index gets one row per logged-in group with <code>is_on_sale=1</code> for product 47. Guests see no change (group 0 is excluded from the rule). On the category page, logged-in customers see "Yes (1)" in the sidebar; guests see only "No (48)".
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-groups">3. Per-customer-group behavior</h2>

    <p>
        Every index row is keyed on <code>(entity_id, customer_group_id, website_id)</code>.
        A single product can be "Yes" for Wholesale but "No" for Guest if the underlying
        rule is scoped that way. The storefront reads the current shopper's group from the
        HTTP Context (populated by Magento's <code>ContextPlugin</code>), which means:
    </p>

    <table class="psf-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Shopper state</th>
                <th>Group ID read from</th>
                <th>Filter result for a rule scoped to "Wholesale only"</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Not logged in</td>
                <td><code>0</code> (NOT LOGGED IN)</td>
                <td><em>No</em> &mdash; the rule doesn't apply to group 0.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Logged in, General (group 1)</td>
                <td><code>1</code></td>
                <td><em>No</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Logged in, Wholesale (group 2)</td>
                <td><code>2</code></td>
                <td><strong>Yes</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Logged in, Retailer (group 3)</td>
                <td><code>3</code></td>
                <td><em>No</em></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="psf-callout psf-callout-info">
        <strong>Why HTTP Context and not Customer Session?</strong> Magento's <code>DepersonalizePlugin</code> resets the customer session to guest before cacheable pages render, so reading <code>customerSession-&gt;getCustomerGroupId()</code> during category rendering always returns <code>0</code>. The HTTP Context is populated earlier in the dispatch chain and survives depersonalization, so v1.0.9+ uses it as the authoritative source for the filter's group id.
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-cache">4. Cache behavior</h2>

    <h3>Full Page Cache keying (built-in FPC)</h3>
    <p>
        Magento 2.4.7 introduced <code>IdentifierForSave</code> which computes the
        FPC cache key purely from <code>$context-&gt;getVaryString()</code>. That value
        is empty at LOAD time (before the controller's <code>beforeExecute</code>
        plugins run), so out of the box every customer group lands on the same
        cache entry &mdash; whichever user warms it wins. This module restores the
        pre-2.4.7 cookie-aware keying via an around-plugin on the FPC Identifier,
        so each group gets its own FPC entry.
    </p>

    <table class="psf-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Event</th>
                <th>Guest entry</th>
                <th>Wholesale entry</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Guest visits /gear/bags.html (first)</td>
                <td>MISS -> stored</td>
                <td>&mdash;</td>
                <td>No cookie yet, default key.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Guest revisits</td>
                <td>HIT</td>
                <td>&mdash;</td>
                <td>Served from FPC.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Customer logs in -> visits /gear/bags.html</td>
                <td>HIT (unchanged)</td>
                <td>MISS -> stored</td>
                <td>Their X-Magento-Vary cookie makes the FPC key differ.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Customer revisits</td>
                <td>HIT</td>
                <td>HIT</td>
                <td>Both entries warm and independent.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Admin saves a catalog rule</td>
                <td>MISS</td>
                <td>MISS</td>
                <td><code>TagInvalidator</code> cleans <code>cat_p</code> + <code>panth_salefilter</code> across every cache frontend, evicting both.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Block-level cache</h3>
    <p>
        The <code>FilterRenderer</code> block implements <code>IdentityInterface</code> and
        emits <code>cat_p</code> + <code>panth_salefilter</code> tags, so any product save,
        catalog rule save/delete, or an explicit call to
        <code>CacheTypeList::cleanType('full_page')</code> evicts its cached HTML.
        Its <code>getCacheKeyInfo()</code> includes the customer group id (from HTTP
        Context), store id, website id, category id, currency, and the active
        <code>sale_filter</code> request parameter &mdash; so per-group per-category
        variants are cached separately.
    </p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-indexer">5. Indexer modes &amp; reindex triggers</h2>

    <table class="psf-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Mode</th>
                <th>When used</th>
                <th>What fires reindex</th>
                <th>Trade-off</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Update on Save</strong></td>
                <td>Small-to-mid catalog, real-time storefront accuracy.</td>
                <td>Catalog rule save / delete, product save / delete, manual reindex.</td>
                <td>Synchronous &mdash; rule save blocks until reindex completes. Accurate immediately.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Update by Schedule</strong></td>
                <td>Large catalogs, batched cron-driven updates.</td>
                <td>MView queue; <code>indexer_update_all_views</code> cron job processes deltas.</td>
                <td>Non-blocking &mdash; rule save returns immediately. Storefront may lag by one cron tick (default: every minute).</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        Switch mode in <a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getIndexerUrl()) ?>">System &gt; Index Management</a>
        or via CLI:
    </p>

<pre><code>bin/magento indexer:set-mode realtime panth_salefilter_product
bin/magento indexer:set-mode schedule panth_salefilter_product</code></pre>

    <h3>Upstream events the module subscribes to</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><code>catalogrule_rule_save_commit_after</code> &mdash; fires on catalog rule save. Uses <em>commit_after</em> (not <em>save_after</em>) because Magento rebuilds <code>catalogrule_product_price</code> in a commit callback; listening earlier would race stale data.</li>
        <li><code>catalogrule_rule_delete_commit_after</code> &mdash; same reason.</li>
        <li><code>catalog_product_save_after</code> &mdash; reindexes just the one product row (realtime mode) or lets MView handle it (schedule mode).</li>
        <li><code>catalog_product_delete_after</code> &mdash; removes the product's rows from the index.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="psf-callout psf-callout-danger">
        <strong>Heads up &mdash; mview races.</strong> If your <code>catalogrule_rule</code> indexer is on <em>Update by Schedule</em> but <code>panth_salefilter_product</code> is on <em>Update on Save</em>, the module forces a synchronous rebuild of <code>catalogrule_rule</code> before reindexing itself, so the sale filter sees fresh rule-price data. You don't need to match their modes.
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="psf-storefront">6. Storefront rendering</h2>

    <p>The filter shows up in two forms depending on the active theme:</p>

    <div class="psf-grid2">
        <div class="psf-card">
            <h3>Luma / default theme</h3>
            <p>
                Rendered by Magento's stock layered-nav block with the theme's
                filter template. Appears as a collapsible "Sale Status" section
                in the sidebar with "Yes" / "No" options and counts.
            </p>
