    <h2 id="psf-troubleshooting">10. Troubleshooting &amp; FAQ</h2>

    <h3>The filter option doesn't appear on the storefront</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Verify the module is enabled: <code>bin/magento module:status Panth_SaleFilter</code></li>
        <li>Open the <a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getGridUrl()) ?>">Sale Filter Index grid</a> and confirm non-zero rows for the website + group you're testing.</li>
        <li>Flush the <em>Layouts</em> and <em>Page Cache</em> types after first install: <code>bin/magento cache:clean layout full_page</code></li>
        <li>Confirm the filter is enabled in <a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getConfigUrl()) ?>">Configuration</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>"Yes" count is 0 but I have active catalog rules</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Check that the rule's <em>Customer Groups</em> and <em>Websites</em> include the scope you're browsing under.</li>
        <li>Check the rule's <em>From Date / To Date</em> window covers <em>today</em>.</li>
        <li>Trigger the built-in catalog-rule indexer: <code>bin/magento indexer:reindex catalogrule_rule</code></li>
        <li>Then our indexer: <code>bin/magento indexer:reindex panth_salefilter_product</code></li>
        <li>Inspect the index grid for the product ids the rule should have discounted. If they're missing, the catalog rule didn't apply (usually a conditions / group / website scope mismatch).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Logged-in customer sees the guest's filter counts</h3>
    <p>
        Fixed in <strong>v1.0.10</strong>. The FPC Identifier plugin now mixes the
        X-Magento-Vary cookie into the cache key so each group gets its own cache
        entry. If you're still seeing the old behavior, upgrade the module and
        run <code>bin/magento cache:flush</code> once to reset pre-upgrade entries.
    </p>

    <h3>Count displayed in sidebar doesn't match the product grid</h3>
    <p>
        Fixed in <strong>v1.0.14</strong>. The sidebar &ldquo;On Sale (N)&rdquo;
        count now mirrors every constraint the grid applies, so the number next
        to &ldquo;Yes&rdquo; always equals the total the shopper would see after
        clicking it:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Stock filter (show_out_of_stock):</strong> when
            <em>Stores &gt; Configuration &gt; Catalog &gt; Inventory &gt; Display Out of Stock Products</em>
            is set to <em>No</em> (Magento default), the count excludes out-of-stock
            products. When set to <em>Yes</em>, OOS rows are included. Works
            identically in both modes.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Other active layered-nav filters:</strong> Brand, Pattern,
            Color, Size, Price range, manual category drill-down &mdash; each
            active sibling filter narrows the &ldquo;On Sale&rdquo; count to the
            intersection. E.g. on
            <code>?pattern=196</code> the sidebar shows &ldquo;On Sale (5)&rdquo;
            when only 5 pattern-196 products are on sale.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Super-attributes on configurables:</strong> when a filter
            attribute (e.g. <code>color</code>) lives on child SKUs, the count
            resolves via <code>catalog_product_index_eav</code> expanded through
            <code>catalog_product_super_link</code> so configurable parents
            whose children match are counted &mdash; matching the grid's ES
            behaviour.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Pagination:</strong> sidebar count is invariant across
            <code>?p=1</code>, <code>?p=2</code>, &hellip; it reflects the full
            filtered total, not the current page slice.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cache &amp; indexer modes:</strong> same count on cold and
            warm FPC, same count under both <em>Update on Save</em> (realtime)
            and <em>Update on Schedule</em> indexer modes &mdash; the read path
            queries <code>panth_salefilter_product_index</code>, so whatever
            state the indexer is in determines the count consistently.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        If you still see a mismatch after upgrading to v1.0.14, run
        <code>bin/magento cache:flush</code> once and
        <code>bin/magento indexer:reindex panth_salefilter_product</code>
        to refresh the index and clear any pre-upgrade FPC entries.
    </p>

    <h3>Do I need to switch to Varnish for per-group caching?</h3>
    <p>
        No. The built-in FPC works correctly with the v1.0.10 identifier plugin.
        Varnish is still recommended for throughput on large-catalog stores, but
        the correctness part of per-group caching is handled at the Magento
        application layer.
    </p>

